    883:| §P.P2 batched π_h(x) on h, slot 1 — does the parallel-h Eratosthenes sieve produce per-h amortised polylog cost as M = #h grows, or does it bottom out at Θ(x/log x) (P1-shape)? | EXPERIMENT (B-NEGATIVE on EXACT regime; B-POSITIVE on APPROXIMATE regime — Thread-7-shape transposed to h-axis) | E (E1.5 + S224 + S231 + S240..S244 — per-h amortised cost = Θ(x/log x) for batched-sieve; HL polylog-per-h with √x error matches Hardy-Littlewood prediction) | Thread 8 commit step 1/4 (S418). Built `experiments/analytic/batched_pi_h/slot1_baseline.py` (~280 lines) implementing three estimators of π_h(x) := #{p prime, p ≤ x : p+h prime} for h ∈ {2, 4, ..., H}: M1 BATCHED SIEVE (sieve [1, x+H] once, for each prime p ≤ x test all H/2 candidates p+h), M2 PER-h SIEVE (single-h timing × M for prediction), M3 HARDY-LITTLEWOOD APPROX (HL_h(x) = S_h · li_2(x), S_h = 2 C_2 Π_{p|h, p odd} (p−1)/(p−2), li_2 via scipy.integrate.quad). **Three-anchor table** at x = 10⁵ / 10⁶ / 10⁷ with H = ⌊log²x⌋ even-rounded capped at 200, M = 66 / 95 / 100: T_batched = 0.041 / 0.438 / 3.893 s ⇒ per-h amort = 0.62 / 4.61 / 38.9 ms. M1/M2 batched-vs-per-h speedup at x = 10⁵, 10⁶: 6.71×, 8.85× — **bounded constant, not growing with M**. Per-h amort ratio across decades: 7.7×, 8.5× ≈ π(x) ratios 8.18×, 8.47× — **per-h amortised cost is Θ(π(x)) = Θ(x/log x), NOT polylog**. **H-sweep at x = 10⁶** with H ∈ {20, 50, 100, 200, 400}, M ∈ {10, 25, 50, 100, 200}: per-h amortised = 8.7 → 6.0 → 5.0 → 4.5 → 4.3 ms — clear floor at ~4 ms (set by per-prime per-h test cost: π(10⁶) · 50 ns ≈ 4 ms). **Per-h cost is M-INDEPENDENT for M ≥ 50.** **HL approximation per-h cost** = 0.62 / 1.23 / 0.68 µs — five orders of magnitude faster than batched-sieve, flat in x and M (polylog shape). **HL approximation accuracy**: mean |π_h − HL_h|/√x ≤ 0.10, max ≤ 0.25 across all 261 (x, H, h) cells; matches Hardy-Littlewood's O(√x) conjectural bound. **Structural dichotomy** (the slot's main result): EXACT regime per-h cost = Θ(x/log x) with zero error (matches P1 / Thread 6 negative shape on the h-axis); APPROXIMATE regime per-h cost = Θ(polylog x) with ≤ 0.25 √x worst error (matches P3 / Thread 7 positive shape on the h-axis). **P2 is Thread-7 (P3) shape, NOT Thread-5 (S224) shape**: the cross-h amortisation in EXACT regime gives only a constant sieve-sharing factor (the h-axis is not a "correlation-of-x" axis in the relevant sense). **What this rules out**: (i) batched-sieve algorithm achieving o(x/log x) per-h amortised for any unbounded M family — directly tested at M ∈ {10..200} and ruled out; (ii) Thread-5-style cross-h correlation amortisation matching the 33× S224 speedup — does not materialise; (iii) the original P2 entry's "shared-h-singular-series structure" being interpreted as exact-cost amortisation — the shared structure helps only the polylog HL approximation, not the EXACT count. **What this does NOT close**: (i) non-sieve algorithms (GPY-weight / Maynard) for batched EXACT π_h — outside slot-1 scope; (ii) quantum batched (P9 shape); (iii) the precise named-exponent error bound for HL approximation — slot 2/3/4 candidate; (iv) the Q-truncation tradeoff for S_Q(h) → S_h — slot 3 candidate. **Pivoted A-grade target for slots 2-4**: precise named-exponent error bound for HL approximation, analogue of Thread 7 Corollary B but for π_h(x). **Cross-domain ingredient**: Hardy-Littlewood k-tuple conjecture (1923) + singular series. CROSS_DOMAIN_TECHNIQUES.md status unchanged (singular series already USED-E in `sanity_singular_series.py`). **Cites** E1.5 (per-h sample-complexity argument), E6.x (sieve hierarchy primitive), S224 (Correlation Dichotomy template — NOT replicated for h-axis), S231 (Thread 6 P1 wrap — negative shape this slot matches in EXACT regime), S240-S244 (Thread 7 P3 — positive shape this slot matches in APPROX regime). See `experiments/analytic/batched_pi_h/{slot1_baseline.py, slot1_baseline_results.md, slot1_raw.tsv, slot1_samples.tsv}`, `archive/sessions/session418_commit_p2_baseline.md`. | 418 |
