    878	| §P.P3 polylog approximation of pi(x) with named-exponent error, slot 3 — do compactly-supported smoothing kernels (Hann, Hamming, Triangle, Riesz, Riesz4, Tukey-25, Tukey-50, Cosine) beat hard cutoff for sigma_eff at fixed K_compute? Resolves the S202 wrap §"Non-Gaussian smoothing kernels" legitimate-falsifier listing | EXPERIMENT (negative-shape closure of the kernel-axis falsifier of slot-1 named-exponent corollary; empirical L2-optimality of hard cutoff for the partial-sum approximation) | E (E1.5 + S195 + S196 + S202 + S240 + S241) — confirms hard cutoff is L2-optimal, no compactly-supported kernel reduces sigma_eff | Thread 7 commit step 3/5 (S242). Built `experiments/analytic/polylog_approx_pi/smoothed_kernels.py` (~340 lines): per-sample cumulative loop computing c_k = 2 Re R(x^{rho_k}) for k=1..K_max=4000 (one R_at_rho call per (sample, k)), then numpy-dot post-processing for each (kernel, K_compute) combination — 9 kernels, 4 K-targets, 20 paired samples, 3 anchors x in {10^7, 10^8, 10^9}. Kernels: hard (w_k=1), triangle (1-u), hann ((1+cos pi u)/2), hamming (0.54+0.46 cos pi u), riesz (1-u^2), riesz4 ((1-u^2)^2), tukey25 (hard for u<=0.75 then cosine taper), tukey50 (hard for u<=0.5 then cosine taper), cosine (cos(pi u/2)) where u = (k-1)/(K-1) in [0,1]. **Headline grid (108 cells, 2160 (anchor, x, K, kernel) data triples)**: zero (anchor, K, kernel) cells where sigma_eff(kernel) < sigma_eff(hard) at paired sign-test p < 0.05 (one-sided). Mean sigma_eff/sigma_eff(hard) across 12 (anchor, K) cells, by kernel: tukey25=1.04, tukey50=1.11, cosine=1.14, riesz=1.12, triangle=1.23, riesz4=1.21, hamming=1.20, hann=1.23 (all > 1). **Decisive hard wins** at 10^8 K=2000+4000 and 10^9 K=2000+4000: ratios 1.06 to 1.62, paired sign test 12-17/20 hard wins, p < 0.01 in 4 cells. **Sub-1 ratios** at K=500 and one tukey25 cell at K=1000 (10^7): all within 1-sigma noise (CV(sigma_eff) at N=20 is ~16%); none significant (smallest p_kernel_wins = 0.13 at 10^7/K=1000/tukey25). Asymmetry: 16 of 24 ratios >= 1.05 are 1-sigma above 1.0; 0 of 9 sub-1 ratios are 1-sigma below 1.0. **Closes S202 wrap §"Non-Gaussian smoothing kernels" legitimate-falsifier**: S196 closed log-Gaussian (multiplicative damping w_j=exp(-h^2 gamma_j^2/2), all j), S202 wrap explicitly listed compactly-supported tapers as "should generalise but not formally proven". Slot 3 directly tests and confirms — 0 of 96 cells show kernel-beats-hard at p<0.05. **Structural reason**: under random-phase Var(error) = sum_j (1-w_j)^2 Var_j is minimised at w_j=1 (j<=K), 0 (j>K), the hard cutoff. The 0.74 GUE factor scales sigma_eff uniformly across kernels (verified empirically: 5 of 5 anchor/K cells with K >= log^2(x) and hard show sigma_eff/sigma_pred in [0.62, 0.85]); GUE corrections do NOT discriminate between kernels at the second-moment level. **Implication for slot-1 named-exponent corollary**: the bound sigma ~ sqrt(x) log K / (pi sqrt(2K) log x) is kernel-optimal in this family; to beat it requires either (a) non-symmetric / position-correlated kernels (paired weights w_{2j-1}=w_{2j} exploiting Wigner repulsion; slot 4 candidate); (b) non-linear post-processing of {c_k} (empirical Bayes shrinkage; outside slot-3 family); (c) move beyond explicit-formula partial-sum framework entirely (slot-5 territory with rigorous variance proof). Slot 3 closes the kernel-optimisation pathway as a route to A-grade. **Cross-domain ingredient**: window-function theory (Bartlett, Hann, Hamming, Tukey, Riesz from signal processing); CROSS_DOMAIN_TECHNIQUES.md should reflect "compactly-supported window functions for explicit-formula partial-sum smoothing — USED-E (S242) — confirmed no improvement over hard cutoff for the partial-sum approximation pi_K(x)". **Falsifier**: (1) a compactly-supported kernel yielding sigma_eff(kernel)/sigma_eff(hard) < 0.95 with paired sign-test p<0.01 at any (anchor, K) cell — directly tested, ruled out; (2) non-symmetric / position-correlated kernel structure — slot-4 candidate; (3) second-moment GUE correction calculation showing kernel-dependent cross-zero covariance — theoretical, outside current machinery. **Cites** E1.5, E3.1, S195, S196, S202, S240, S241. See `experiments/analytic/polylog_approx_pi/{smoothed_kernels.py, smoothed_kernels_results.md, smoothed_kernels_data.csv (2160 rows), smoothed_kernels_summary.csv (108 rows), smoothed_kernels_main.log, smoothed_kernels_paired_analysis.py, smoothed_kernels_paired.log}`, `archive/sessions/session242_commit_p3_smoothed_kernels.md`. | 242 |
