s within +/-5%). **eps/sqrt(x) at K=8000 fixed, 1/log x decay**: 9.74e-4 (10^7) -> 8.84e-4 (10^8) -> 7.81e-4 (10^9), cross-decade ratio 1.25 vs predicted log(10^9)/log(10^7) = 1.286 — agreement within 3%. **Empirical anomaly at small K**: logx policy (K = 17, 19, 22) gives sigma_eff/sigma_pred = {0.734, 0.599, 0.816} much noisier than larger-K policies — expected because random-phase asymptotic regime breaks at K below ~50 (sigma_pred drops the K small-zero terms wholesale). **Theoretical extrapolation table** (sigma_pred for K = log^alpha x at x in {10^6, 10^8, 10^10, 10^12, 10^15, 10^18, 10^21, 10^24}, alpha in {2, 3, 4, 6, 8}): at x = 10^15 with K = log^4(10^15) ~ 9.3e5 zeros, sigma/sqrt(x) ~ 7.7e-5 -> polylog-time algorithm with eps ~ 2400 vs R(x) baseline O(sqrt(x)) ~ 3.2e7 (13000x improvement, heuristic). **Distinction from slot 1**: slot 1 was 5 single-anchor data points; slot 2 is 90 multi-sample points = 360 (x, policy) data triples. Slot 1 measured median ratio 0.476; slot 2 confirms half-Gaussian shape (KS_p_eff median 0.69) AND extends to one new decade (10^9, no prior data; S195 stopped at 10^7). **Falsifiability**: (1) multi-sample run at x >= 10^12 with KS_p_eff < 0.01 across multiple policies; (2) rigorous proof of GUE pair-correlation factor < 0.74^2 = 0.55 (would imply sigma_eff smaller than measured); (3) K-policy below logx where asymptotic formula breaks measurably (already documented at K = 17-22). **No A-grade**: still validates S195's heuristic; doesn't produce a new theorem or beat an algorithm; the partial-positive shape is unchanged from slot 1, just with more confidence and one new decade. **Cross-domain ingredient**: GUE pair-correlation distribution (Montgomery 1973, Odlyzko 1989) extended from USED-E (S195) to USED-E + USED-I (slot 2 inverts to give algorithmic ratio bound). CROSS_DOMAIN_TECHNIQUES.md unchanged but slot 2 anchor row strengthens the empirical evidence. **Cites** E1.5 (per-query bit-content barrier; sigma_eff bounds typical error log-factor below sqrt(x)), E2.1, E3.1 (Thread 3 closure transitivity now backed by 360-point data extending to 10^9), S195 (variance formula re-validated at 10^9), S196 (Galway frontier closure conditional on same heuristic), S202 (unified CCM closure), S224 (Correlation Dichotomy template Thread 7 follows), S240 (slot 1 named-exponent corollary). **Slot 3 next-action**: push empirical anchor to x = 10^12 via memory-efficient sieve (bit-packed segmented sieve, or primality-test-only approach in narrow sample windows where pi(anchor) is precomputable); OR pivot to slot 4 (smoothed kernel selection: Gaussian / raised-cosine kernels — do they reduce eps beyond unsmoothed sum?). See `experiments/analytic/polylog_approx_pi/{multi_sample.py, multi_sample_results.md, multi_sample_data.csv, multi_sample_summary.csv, multi_sample_main.log}`, `archive/sessions/session241_commit_p3_multisample.md`. | 241 |
    880	| §P.P3 polylog approximation of pi(x) with named-exponent error, slot 4 — do non-symmetric / position-correlated ("paired") kernel weights w_{2j-1} != w_{2j} (or paired-resolution / boundary-only / antipair perturbations) beat hard cutoff for sigma_eff at fixed K_compute? Resolves the only remaining kernel-axis falsifier of slot-1 named-exponent corollary not addressed by S196 (log-Gaussian) or S242 (symmetric compactly-supported) | EXPERIMENT (negative-shape closure of the paired-kernel-axis falsifier; empirical L2-optimality of hard cutoff extends from symmetric (S242) to non-symmetric / position-correlated kernel families) | E (E1.5 + S195 + S196 + S202 + S240 + S241 + S242) — confirms hard cutoff is L2-optimal across ALL kernel families tested; F_GUE = 0.55 ± 0.06 stable across 17 kernels (8 symmetric + 7 paired + hard) | Thread 7 commit step 4/5 (S243). Built `experiments/analytic/polylog_approx_pi/paired_kernels.py` (~430 lines): per-sample cumulative loop computing c_k = 2 Re R(x^{rho_k}) for k=1..K_max=4000, then numpy-dot post-processing for each (kernel, K_compute) — 7 paired families + hard baseline, 4 K-targets, 20 paired samples, 3 anchors x in {10^7, 10^8, 10^9}. Built-in paired-sign-test post-processor. Kernels: hard (w_k=1), paired_hann (w_k = hann at pair resolution, w_{2j-1}=w_{2j}=hann((j-1)/(K/2-1))), paired_triangle (triangle at pair resolution), paired_riesz (Riesz at pair resolution), antipair_03 (w_{2j-1}=1.3, w_{2j}=0.7), antipair_05 (w_{2j-1}=1.5, w_{2j}=0.5), half_int (w_k=1 for k<K, w_K=0.5 — half-integer cutoff), boundary_pair (w_k=1 for k<K-1, w_{K-1}=0.75, w_K=0.25 — taper applied only to boundary pair). **Headline grid (96 summary cells, 1920 (anchor, x, K, kernel) per-sample triples)**: ZERO (anchor, K, kernel) cells where sigma_eff(kernel) < sigma_eff(hard) at paired sign-test p < 0.05 (one-sided). Smallest p_kernel_beats_hard across all 84 cells = 0.2517 (boundary_pair, 10^7 K=500, wins=12/20 — within 1-sigma noise of break-even). Mean sigma_eff/sigma_eff(hard) across 12 (anchor, K) cells, by kernel: boundary_pair=0.999, half_int=0.999, paired_riesz=1.12, paired_triangle=1.23, paired_hann=1.23, antipair_03=2.12, antipair_05=3.24. **Decisive HARD wins** at 17 cells with p_hard_beats_kernel < 0.05: antipair_05 in 7+ cells with p<0.01 (catastrophic at K=4000, 10^9: sigma_eff = 240 vs hard 47.5, ratio 5.05×); antipair_03 at 10^9 K>=1000 with p ≈ 0.001-0.002; paired_hann/triangle at 10^9 K=1000 + 10^8 K>=2000 with p in 0.006-0.021. **Boundary-only kernels (half_int, boundary_pair) are statistically indistinguishable from hard**: max |Delta(sigma_eff/sigma_eff_hard)| = 0.6%, all 24 cells within 1-sigma noise of break-even. The head-term penalty (1-w_K)^2 Var_K = 0.25 Var_K for half_int (resp. 0.625 Var for boundary_pair) is one zero's worth of variance, swamped by the tail Sum_{j>K} Var_j ≈ K Var_K log^2 K — half a zero's variance is ~1% of total variance, below detection at N=20. **Antipair scaling matches random-phase L2 prediction exactly**: Var(antipair) - Var(hard) = (1-w)^2 K Var_avg with (1-w_k)^2 = 0.09 for antipair_03 and 0.25 for antipair_05; observed sigma_eff^2 / sigma_eff_hard^2 ratios at K=4000 are 4.5 (antipair_03) and 25.5 (antipair_05), consistent with adding 0.09 K v_avg / 0.25 K v_avg to the residual head term once K saturates the tail. **Closes the only remaining kernel-axis falsifier of the Thread 3 closure**: S196 closed log-Gaussian (multiplicative damping); S242 closed symmetric compactly-supported; S243 closes non-symmetric / paired / boundary-only / antipair. Together: HARD CUTOFF IS EMPIRICALLY L2-OPTIMAL ACROSS ALL TESTED KERNEL FAMILIES (17 kernels, 180 (anchor, K, kernel) cells across slots 3+4). **GUE pair-correlation is kernel-invariant at second-moment level**: F_GUE := sigma_eff^2 / sigma_pred^2 = 0.55 ± 0.06 across all 17 kernels (cv ≈ 11%). The L2-optimality of hard transfers verbatim to the GUE-corrected regime. **Implication for slot-1 named-exponent corollary**: the bound sigma ≈ sqrt(x) log K / (pi sqrt(2K) log x) is *kernel-optimal* in the linear partial-sum framework. Remaining levers for A-grade are (a) non-linear post-processing (empirical Bayesian shrinkage; outside linear-kernel framework), (b) adversarial K-policy selection per-x, or (c) rigorous variance bound under Montgomery's pair-correlation conjecture (slot-5 territory). **Cross-domain ingredient**: random matrix theory pair-correlation (Wigner kernel K_W(t) = sin^2(pi t)/(pi t)^2). The fact that paired/antipair kernels do not exploit this for L2 reduction is consistent with K_W being EVEN — sign-pattern manipulation cancels at second moment. Higher-order GUE statistics (3rd moment) might break this, but slot 4 confirms second-moment kernel design is closed. **Falsifier**: (1) any kernel with sigma_eff/sigma_eff_hard < 0.95 at p < 0.01 — directly tested across 84 cells, ruled out (best 0.97 at 10^9 K=500 paired_riesz, p=0.41); (2) a kernel with non-uniform F_GUE (kernel-dependent GUE-correction) that crosses sigma_eff(hard) — empirically F_GUE within +/-5% across all 17 kernels; (3) higher-order GUE statistics (3rd moment) — outside linear partial-sum framework. **Cites** E1.5 (per-query bit-content barrier), E3.1 (Thread 3 closure transitivity), S195 (sigma-formula), S196 (log-Gaussian closure), S202 (unified CCM closure §"Non-Gaussian smoothing kernels"), S240 (slot 1 named-exponent corollary), S241 (slot 2 multi-sample distribution test), S242 (slot 3 symmetric-kernel closure). **Slot 5 next-action**: theoretical wrap via rigorous variance bound under Montgomery's pair-correlation conjecture. Convert heuristic named-exponent corollary to unconditional theorem under Montgomery; if obstruction documented, close Thread 7 as DONE_PARTIAL_POSITIVE_HEURISTIC with the named-exponent corollary as the partial-positive result and 17 kernel families as negative-shape evidence of L2-optimality. See `experiments/analytic/polylog_approx_pi/{paired_kernels.py,